Hashes are one of the MOST important data structures in programming and web development.

If arrays are:

```text id="db4gkq"
collections of items
```

then hashes are:

```text id="j25tt1"
organized data with labels
```

A hash stores data using:

```text id="skxvjk"
key => value
```

or:

```ruby id="p6xzwg"
name: "Ashoor"
```

This is HUGE in real software engineering because almost all real-world data is structured like this.

---

# Real Project Examples

---

# Example 1 — User Data

You practiced:

```ruby id="h2b0m4"
person = {
  "first_name" => "Michael",
  "last_name" => "Jordan"
}
```

Real backend example:

```ruby id="v0u7t3"
user = {
  name: "Ashoor",
  email: "ashoor@gmail.com",
  role: "developer"
}
```

That’s how applications think about users.

---

# Example 2 — API Responses

This is MASSIVE.

Most APIs return hashes (or JSON objects).

Real API response:

```json id="e3myy1"
{
  "title": "Software Engineer",
  "company": "Google",
  "salary": 120000
}
```

In Ruby/Rails:

```ruby id="k0mqpn"
job = {
  title: "Software Engineer",
  company: "Google",
  salary: 120000
}
```

That’s literally the same thing you’re practicing.

---

# Example 3 — Database Records

In Rails:

```ruby id="n5qlc5"
@user.name
@user.email
```

Underneath, Rails models are basically structured collections of attributes like hashes.

Your practice is teaching you how structured data works.

