# Example 6 — Arrays of Hashes

This is EXTREMELY important.

You practiced:

```ruby id="zwtq7w"
people = [
  {"first_name" => "Mike"},
  {"first_name" => "Sam"}
]
```

This is basically how:

* databases
* APIs
* frontend data
* dashboards

work.

Example:

```ruby id="k5ih5f"
jobs = [
  {title: "Engineer", company: "Google"},
  {title: "Developer", company: "Apple"}
]
```

That’s real-world backend/frontend data structure.

---

# Why Arrays of Hashes Matter So Much

Because most applications deal with:

```text id="i6uqm4"
multiple objects that each have attributes
```

Example:

* multiple users
* multiple jobs
* multiple products
* multiple messages

That becomes:

```ruby id="snwnn6"
[
  {name: "Mike", age: 25},
  {name: "Sarah", age: 30}
]
```

This is one of the MOST common structures in all programming.

---

# Real React Example

Your frontend may receive:

```javascript id="l9m4np"
const jobs = [
  { title: "Engineer", company: "Google" },
  { title: "Designer", company: "Apple" }
];
```

Then React loops through them:

```javascript id="d5bz12"
jobs.map((job) => (
  <JobCard title={job.title} />
))
```

That entire flow depends on:

* arrays
* hashes/objects

Exactly what you’re practicing.

