# What Your Exercises Are Teaching

---

# Reading Data

You practiced:

```ruby id="qjlwm9"
person["first_name"]
```

Real app usage:

```ruby id="snj1by"
user[:email]
```

This teaches:

* accessing structured data
* retrieving values

---

# Adding Data

You practiced:

```ruby id="a4x2wx"
states[:California] = "Sacramento"
```

Real app usage:

```ruby id="8n7bsl"
user[:role] = "admin"
```

or:

```ruby id="cvt2ul"
settings[:dark_mode] = true
```

This teaches mutation and dynamic updates.   Mutation - means changing the original object/data directly; instead of creating a brand new copy.
                                             Dynamic Updates - means changing data while the program is running; instead of hardcoding everything ahead of time.
---

# Organizing Data

Instead of random variables:

```ruby id="wz9m3f"
name = "Ashoor"
email = "test@gmail.com"
role = "developer"
```

you organize them:

```ruby id="lc7qiw"
user = {
  name: "Ashoor",
  email: "test@gmail.com",
  role: "developer"
}
```

That’s MUCH more scalable.   Scalable - something can continue working well even as it grows bigger; can handle - more users, data, traffic, features WITHOUT breaking or becoming messy.

Real applications organize data like this constantly.

