# Biggest Picture

Hashes teach:

* structured data
* object thinking
* attribute relationships
* backend/API thinking
* scalable organization

This is one of the deepest transitions from:

> “simple scripts”      Scripts - means a file / program that runs a set of intructions automatically - often - smaller, task-focused, procedural, run from start to finish

to:

> “real application architecture”  Application Architecture - the overall structure and organization of how an application is built

Because real applications are mostly:

* collections of structured data
* being created
* updated
* displayed
* transferred through     APIs  - means -  applications constantly SEND and RECEIVE data between different systems using APIs
                          API - Application Programming Interface - a messenger between systems
                          Ex. React frontend
                            ↓ request
                          Rails backend API
                            ↓
                          PostgreSQL database = React(frontend) retrieving data by sending request to Rails(backend API - system) -> then reaching the database(PostgreSQL) -> rails backend returns JSON (an array of hashes like you've practiced displaying the PostgreSQL data in JSON format - transferred through the API)
* saved to databases

And hashes are foundational to all of that.
